// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Theme A asks what all living things share and how they differ. It starts at the molecular level.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Water's polarity and hydrogen bonding give it cohesion, adhesion, high specific heat and solvent properties that make life possible."},{"t":"p","text":"Nucleic acids: DNA (deoxyribose, double helix, A–T and C–G complementary base pairing, antiparallel strands) and RNA (ribose, single-stranded, uracil). The genetic code is universal, which is evidence of common ancestry."},{"t":"p","text":"Cell theory: all organisms are made of cells, cells are the smallest unit of life, and cells come from pre-existing cells. Prokaryotes lack a nucleus; eukaryotes have membrane-bound organelles. Endosymbiosis explains the origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Measuring cells under a microscope","aim":"Measure the image of a cell and use the magnification to find its actual size.","sets":[{"name":"I","start":20,"label":"Image size (mm)","min":1,"max":100,"step":1},{"name":"M","start":400,"label":"Magnification","min":40,"max":1000,"step":10}],"outs":[{"name":"A","expr":"I*1000/M","label":"Actual size (μm)"}],"steps":["Record the actual size for 4 magnifications of a 20 mm image.","A typical animal cell is 10–30 μm. Which settings match?"],"noise":0,"result":"Actual size = image size ÷ magnification. Converting units carefully (1 mm = 1,000 μm) is the most common source of error. Eukaryotic cells are usually 10–100 μm; bacteria about 1–5 μm.","plot":{"x":"M","y":"A"}}]},{"say":"Life is incredibly diverse. Biologists classify organisms to understand their relationships.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A species is often defined as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, though this biological species concept has limits (asexual organisms, fossils)."},{"t":"p","text":"Classification uses a hierarchy (domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species) and binomial nomenclature (Homo sapiens). Cladistics groups organisms by shared derived characteristics into clades, often using DNA sequences."},{"t":"p","text":"The three domains: Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya."},{"t":"p","text":"Biodiversity can be measured with richness and evenness; dichotomous keys identify organisms."}]}],[{"say":"Theme B shows how structure determines function, from proteins to whole organ systems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Proteins: amino acids joined by peptide bonds, with primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure. Shape determines function; denaturation destroys it."},{"t":"p","text":"Membranes: the fluid mosaic model, a phospholipid bilayer with proteins and cholesterol. Transport: simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, osmosis (passive) and active transport using ATP and pumps; endocytosis and exocytosis for bulk transport."},{"t":"p","text":"Compartmentalization in eukaryotic cells allows specialized conditions (for example, low pH in lysosomes)."},{"t":"tip","text":"In exam answers, link each structure to its function: say what the feature is, then explain how it helps the cell or organism."}]},{"say":"Organisms have evolved specialized structures to exchange gases and transport materials efficiently.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Gas exchange surfaces are thin, moist, have a large surface area and a good blood supply. In humans, alveoli and ventilation (diaphragm and intercostal muscles) maintain concentration gradients. In leaves, stomata allow gas exchange."},{"t":"p","text":"Blood vessels: arteries (thick, elastic walls, high pressure), veins (thin walls, valves) and capillaries (one cell thick for exchange)."},{"t":"p","text":"Plants transport water through xylem by transpiration (cohesion–tension) and sugars through phloem by translocation."},{"t":"tip","text":"Fick's law sums it up: the rate of diffusion increases with surface area and concentration difference, and decreases with the thickness of the barrier."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Potometer and transpiration","aim":"Use a potometer to measure how fast a leafy shoot takes up water in different conditions.","sets":[{"name":"RH","start":50,"label":"Relative humidity (%)","min":20,"max":95,"step":5},{"name":"w","start":1,"label":"Wind speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":5,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"rate","expr":"2*(1 - RH/100)*(1 + 0.6*sqrt(w))","label":"Water uptake (mm per min)"}],"steps":["Record the rate at 5 humidity levels in still air.","Then add a fan and compare."],"noise":5,"result":"Transpiration is fastest in dry, windy conditions because the water vapor gradient between the leaf and the air is steepest. Water loss pulls water up the xylem by cohesion–tension.","plot":{"x":"RH","y":"rate"}}]}],[{"say":"Theme C explores how molecules and organisms interact. Energy flow through cells is central.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Enzymes lower activation energy; their activity depends on temperature, pH and substrate concentration. Competitive inhibitors bind the active site; non-competitive inhibitors bind elsewhere. End-product inhibition regulates pathways."},{"t":"p","text":"Cell respiration releases energy from glucose to make ATP: anaerobic respiration (glycolysis plus fermentation, small ATP yield) and aerobic respiration (much more ATP using oxygen)."},{"t":"p","text":"Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy: light-dependent reactions produce ATP and NADPH and release O₂; the Calvin cycle fixes CO₂ into carbohydrates. Limiting factors: light intensity, CO₂ concentration and temperature."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Yeast fermentation and sugar","aim":"Give yeast different glucose concentrations and measure how fast it produces CO₂.","sets":[{"name":"S","start":2,"label":"Glucose (%)","min":0,"max":20,"step":0.5},{"name":"Vm","start":10,"label":"Maximum rate (cm³ CO₂/min)","min":5,"max":20,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"v","expr":"Vm*S/(1.5 + S)","label":"CO₂ production (cm³/min)"}],"steps":["Record the rate at 6 glucose concentrations.","Why does adding more sugar eventually stop helping?"],"noise":5,"result":"The rate rises, then levels off as the yeast's enzymes become saturated (all active sites occupied). At high sugar concentrations another factor, the amount of enzyme, becomes limiting.","plot":{"x":"S","y":"v"}}]},{"say":"Organisms maintain internal balance, and they interact in communities and ecosystems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Homeostasis uses negative feedback: blood glucose (insulin lowers it, glucagon raises it), body temperature, and water balance. Cell signaling uses hormones and neurotransmitters binding to receptors."},{"t":"p","text":"Populations: growth curves (exponential and logistic), carrying capacity, and limiting factors. Interactions: competition, predation, herbivory, mutualism and parasitism."},{"t":"p","text":"Ecosystems: energy flows through trophic levels with about 90% lost as heat at each step; nutrients cycle (the carbon cycle). Keystone species have large effects on communities."},{"t":"ex","problem":"After a meal, blood glucose rises. Describe the response.","steps":["Pancreas beta cells secrete insulin"],"answer":"Cells absorb glucose and the liver stores it as glycogen; glucose returns to normal (negative feedback)"}]}],[{"say":"Theme D explains how life continues and changes across generations, starting with genetic information.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"DNA replication is semi-conservative: helicase unwinds, DNA polymerase builds new strands 5′ to 3′. Protein synthesis: transcription (DNA → mRNA) and translation (mRNA → protein at ribosomes, with tRNA anticodons matching codons)."},{"t":"p","text":"Cell division: mitosis produces genetically identical cells for growth and repair; meiosis produces genetically varied haploid gametes through crossing over and independent assortment. Errors (non-disjunction) cause conditions like Down syndrome."},{"t":"p","text":"Inheritance: dominant, recessive and codominant alleles, sex linkage, and multiple alleles (ABO blood groups). Punnett grids predict offspring ratios; chi-squared tests compare observed and expected results."}]},{"say":"Life changes over time through evolution, and today, rapid climate change is affecting ecosystems worldwide.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Natural selection: variation exists, more offspring are produced than survive, individuals with better-adapted traits survive and reproduce more, and those traits become more common. Evidence: fossils, homologous structures, DNA sequences and observed change (antibiotic resistance)."},{"t":"p","text":"Speciation occurs through reproductive isolation (allopatric or sympatric). Gene pools and allele frequencies change through selection, drift and gene flow (HL: Hardy–Weinberg)."},{"t":"p","text":"Climate change: rising CO₂ from fossil fuels enhances the greenhouse effect, causing warming, ocean acidification, shifts in species ranges and coral bleaching. Mitigation includes reducing emissions and protecting carbon sinks."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Antibiotic resistance spreading","aim":"Track the fraction of resistant bacteria in a population when an antibiotic gives them an advantage each generation.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":10,"label":"Generations","min":0,"max":60,"step":1},{"name":"s","start":0.3,"label":"Selective advantage","min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.05}],"outs":[{"name":"p","expr":"0.001*(1 + s)^t/(0.001*(1 + s)^t + 0.999)","label":"Fraction resistant"}],"steps":["Record the fraction resistant every 10 generations.","Lower the selective advantage, as when antibiotics are used less."],"noise":2,"result":"A rare resistant mutant spreads slowly at first, then rapidly, until it dominates (an S-shaped curve). Bacteria divide every 20–30 minutes, so this can happen in days; reducing antibiotic overuse lowers the advantage.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"p"}}]}]];
export default text;
